after months of delays spacex received  word from regulators about the fate of  its starbase launch site it's a pretty  big deal for the company and the faa  basically you know gave the company the  green light to move forward on monday  the faa released its final environmental  assessment of the company's south texas  location the assessment found that  continued launches at the bogochica site  would not significantly affect the  quality of the human environment it was  a big hurdle i mean it created  a fair amount of i guess you call it  regulatory uncertainty it means that  spacex has a pathway forward to start  the kind of testing to show that this  gigantic rocket really can work  the agency's assessment also found that  before it can start orbital launches  spacex must carry out more than 75  actions to mitigate the effects of the  launches on the surrounding area and  environment these actions range from  noise restrictions to monitoring the  lighting at the launch site to preparing  a report on historic events that took  place in the area during the  mexican-american war and the civil war  so what does this assessment mean for  spacex and the starship program going  forward  moreover what does it mean for nasa's  plan to get the us back to the moon by  2025  last year spacex ceo elon musk announced  that he was creating a new city called  star base at boca chica village an  unincorporated community of about 30  homes located next to the southernmost  beach in texas  spacex originally planned to use the  site for launches of its falcon 9 and  falcon heavy rockets but later it  decided to use starbase to develop its  much larger starship spacecraft  spacex has been conducting sub-orbital  starship launches at boca chica since  2019  but for the spaceship's development to  continue the faa requires additional  permitting starship is the the biggest  and most powerful rocket system spacex  has ever developed it's meant for some  of the companies most ambitious deep  space missions  although many in the state welcome the  increased investment and jobs some in  south texas are concerned that spacex's  expansion will come at a steep price to  the environment one of the major  concerns is that the faa hasn't done  enough analysis of what the effects  of operating starship you know from this  location would be  bokeh chica sits at the edge of a  national wildlife refuge but since  spacex began expanding its operations in  2019  some conservationists worry that the  eventual environmental impact will be  devastating  the faa's final environmental assessment  of the boko chica site was expected last  year  however the agency pushed back the  deadline five times saying spacex made  changes to its application that required  additional analysis one of the big  reasons that this has been delayed  multiple times is because  the faa said almost 20 000 people have  weighed in on this and provided public  comment you know for against and they  had to to go through those  the breadth of submissions range from  opposition by a wildlife biologist to  support from an engineer  others were submitted on behalf of  groups and many were pages long  the completion of the assessment does  not guarantee that the faa will issue  spacex a launch license spacex's  application must also meet faa safety  risk and financial requirements spacex  expects the first orbital launch of its  starship spacecraft to take place later  this year  starship is designed for voyages to the  moon and mars and nasa selected starship  to return humans to the moon in 2025.  they can't start launching immediately  it's it's not  quite that simple the company also has  to  make sure it's  you know complying with all of these  these actions  so they still have some some steps to go  before  they start launching spacex did not  respond to multiple requests for comment  but shortly after the faa's decision the  company did tweet that starship was one  step closer to its first orbital flight
 

